<section class="fw-section">
    <div class="fw-col">
        <h2><span class="fw-num">07</span>Where you can watch it run</h2>

        <p>
            No client has reported results back yet, so there are no reply rates or won
            figures on this page. Putting invented ones here would be the exact dishonesty
            the service is sold against. What follows is the work itself, counted.
        </p>

        <h3 class="fw-sub">Two weeks in Accra</h3>

        <p>
            Since August 2026 a delivery and logistics operator in Accra has been receiving a
            weekly list of businesses that move goods to customers often enough to be worth
            calling. Forty businesses have been sent so far across two lists, from frozen food
            and pharmaceutical supply to scaffolding and school books, and no business has been
            sent twice.
        </p>

        <dl class="fw-figures">
            <div>
                <dt>49</dt>
                <dd>businesses sourced in the first week from Google, TikTok and Instagram</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
                <dt>18</dt>
                <dd>of them could be shown to be trading, and were sent</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
                <dt>31</dt>
                <dd>could not, and were not sent</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
                <dt>40</dt>
                <dd>businesses delivered over two weeks, each with a dated signal behind it</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
                <dt>36</dt>
                <dd>of the forty reachable on WhatsApp, which is how this market answers</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
                <dt>0</dt>
                <dd>repeats, tracked against a register of every name ever surfaced</dd>
            </div>
        </dl>

        <p>
            The thirty one that were dropped are the point. Every one of them looked usable in
            a search. They had listings, followers, a website, a phone number. None of them
            could be shown to have done anything in public in the previous thirty days, so
            none of them went on the sheet. That is the work being paid for, and it is the
            part a database cannot do for you.
        </p>

        <h3 class="fw-sub">Where the method was wrong</h3>

        <p>
            In the first week one wholesaler was held back because the newest thing that could
            be found about it was eight months old. In the second week it turned up trading
            actively under a separate wholesale account, posting every fortnight, with four
            branch order lines and shipping to more than a thousand retailers. It was the
            strongest prospect in the whole pool and the first check had missed it.
        </p>

        <p>
            A business can look dead on the profile you find first and be busy on one you have
            not found yet. The fix was to check for a second account before writing anything
            off, and it is now part of the routine. This is on the page because a method that
            never reports its own misses is not a method.
        </p>

        <h3 class="fw-sub">The same machine at full size</h3>

        <p>
            In August 2026 it found 3,928 businesses listed on Google Maps across the UK, the
            Gulf and the US, opened all of them, and checked eight things about each homepage.
            2,828 of the websites loaded at all. 42.9% of those had no HTTPS.
        </p>

        <p>
            That is the same searching, the same checking by hand and the same removal of dead
            ends described in section 03, pointed at a whole market instead of one client's
            definition. The full method, the sample and its limits are on the study, including
            the parts that make the headline look worse.
        </p>

        <p>
            <a class="fw-inline-link" href="/blog/small-business-website-crawl-2026/">
                Read the crawl study
            </a>
        </p>
    </div>
</section>

